ves duplication (the `result.push([x, y])` appears twice, and the liveNeighborCount === 3 check appears twice conceptually - once for alive and once for dead).\n\nAlso, the candidate collection logic duplicates the neighbor offset iteration - once in `countLiveNeighbors` and once in the candidate collection. But that's not really duplication - they serve different purposes.\n\nLet me also consider: could we simplify the candidate collection? Instead of building a candidateSet separately, we could... actually the current approach is clean enough.\n\nLet me focus on the two improvements:\n1. Rename `liveNeighbors` → `liveNeighborCount` (Rule 2)\n2. Consolidate the if/else into a single condition (Rule 3 - DRY, and Rule 4 - fewer elements)\n\nLet me calculate APP mass before and after.\n\nActually, let me think more carefully. The `isAlive` variable is only used in the condition. After simplifying, we might not even need it as a separate variable:\n\n```\nif (liveNeighborCount === 3 || (liveSet.has(key) && liveNeighborCount === 2)) {\n  result.push([x, y]);\n}\n```\n\nBut that's less clear. The `isAlive` variable reveals intent. So let me keep it.\n\nAfter refactoring:\n```\nconst isAlive = liveSet.has(key);\nconst survives = liveNeighborCount === 3 || (isAlive && liveNeighborCount === 2);\nif (survives) {\n  result.push([x, y]);\n}\n```\n\nHmm, adding `survives` adds a binding. But it reveals intent better. Let me weigh:\n- Rule 2 says clarity trumps everything (including APP)\n- The `survives` variable makes the logic self-documenting\n- But is it really clearer than the if/else? The if/else explicitly shows the two cases (alive with 2-3 neighbors, dead with 3 neighbors), which maps directly to the Game of Life rules.\n\nActually, I think the if/else is more aligned with the domain rules. The four rules are:\n1. Underpopulation: alive + < 2 neighbors → dies\n2. Survival: alive + 2-3 neighbors → lives\n3. Overpopulation: alive + > 3 neighbors → dies\n4. Reproduction: dead + exactly 3 neighbors → lives\n\nThe if/else structure maps to these rules. The simplified condition `liveNeighborCount === 3 || (isAlive && liveNeighborCount === 2)` is more concise but less directly maps to the rules.\n\nHowever, there IS duplication: `result.push([x, y])` appears twice. This violates Rule 3.\n\nLet me go with a middle ground - consolidate the branches but keep the condition readable:\n\n```typescript\nif (liveNeighborCount === 3 || (isAlive && liveNeighborCount === 2)) {\n  result.push([x, y]);\n}\n```\n\nThis eliminates the duplicate `result.push([x, y])` and also the conceptual duplication of checking `liveNeighborCount === 3` in both branches.\n\nLet me calculate:\n\n**Before (nextGeneration main loop)**:\n```\nconst liveNeighborCount = countLiveNeighbors(liveSet, x, y);  // binding:1, invocation:1, assignment:1\nconst isAlive = liveSet.has(key);                              // binding:1, invocation:1, assignment:1\nif (isAlive && (liveNeighborCount === 2 || liveNeighborCount === 3)) {  // conditional:1, constants:2 (2,3)\n  result.push([x, y]);                                         // invocation:1\n} else if (!isAlive && liveNeighborCount === 3) {              // conditional:1, constant:1 (3)\n  result.push([x, y]);                                         // invocation:1\n}\n```\n\nSub-mass:\n- Bindings: 2 (liveNeighborCount, isAlive)\n- Invocations: 3 (countLiveNeighbors, has, push × 2)\n- Conditionals: 2 (if, else if)\n- Constants: 3 (2, 3, 3)\n- Assignments: 2\n\n= 2*1 + 3*2 + 2*4 + 3*1 + 2*6 = 2 + 6 + 8 + 3 + 12 = 31\n\n**After**:\n```\nconst liveNeighborCount = countLiveNeighbors(liveSet, x, y);  // binding:1, invocation:1, assignment:1\nconst isAlive = liveSet.has(key);                              // binding:1, invocation:1, assignment:1\nif (liveNeighborCount === 3 || (isAlive && liveNeighborCount === 2)) {  // conditional:1, constants:2 (3,2)\n  result.push([x, y]);                                         // invocation:1\n}\n`